<p>After an upgrade recently, I discovered there was a process called
<code>Siri AI</code> running. I found this odd, since I've explitly turned off
all AI features in macOS, including Siri.</p>
<p>This prompted me to find out how much CPU and memory has this <code>Sire
AI</code> process consumed. I first got the process ID:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code><span class="n">pgrep</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">-</span><span class="n">lf</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">'Siri AI'</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">|</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="n">awk</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">'{print $1}'</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>Then, I read the <code>ps</code> man page to find out how I can list explicit
properties of a given process:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>$  ps -o pid,%cpu,%mem,etime,rss,command -p &lt;pid&gt;
</code></pre></div>

<p>With those two commands in store, I could now combine them to find out
how much this Siri AI process was consuming and how long it had been
running:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code>$ ps -o pid,%cpu,%mem,etime,rss,command -p $(pgrep -lf 'Siri AI' | awk '{print $1}')
  PID  %CPU %MEM     ELAPSED    RSS COMMAND
38430   0.0  0.4 05-21:13:12 212480 /System/Applications/Siri AI.app/Contents/MacOS/Siri AI
</code></pre></div>